Key Data Insights
1. Academic Performance & Well-Being (KPIs)
•	Students spend an average of 8.43 hours per week using generative AI tools.
•	The average change in GPA associated with AI usage is minimal, standing at just +0.20 points.
•	the average exam-related anxiety level is recorded at 4.27 (on an unlabelled scale, likely out of 5 or 10), indicating moderate to high stress.


2. AI Adoption Across Majors
•	: The tree map reveals that STEM and Business majors represent the largest blocks of AI users.
•	 Arts and Medical students utilize AI the least among the tracked categories.
•	Despite different enrollment sizes, the "Category wise weekly AI hours" chart shows that the actual hours spent per student remain uniform (roughly 7–10 hours weekly) across all disciplines.

3. Institutional Policy Response
•	The bar chart tracks student AI usage across three institutional stances: Actively Encouraged, Allowed With Restrictions, and Strict Ban.
•	STEM and Business students maintain high AI usage even in environments with a "Strict Ban" or heavy restrictions, indicating that institutional policies do not entirely deter tool adoption.


4. Subscription Models
•	A significant majority of students (57.69%) utilize paid subscriptions for AI tools.
•	The remaining 42.31% of students rely on free versions of generative AI software.



Recommendations: 
•	institutional policies like "Strict Bans" do not successfully deter student AI adoption, particularly within high-use fields like STEM and Business
•	The institution should investigate campus-wide licensing agreements or subsidized access for academic-grade AI tools. This ensures a level playing field and standardized data privacy protections for all students
•	AI adoption is not uniform across the student body. Academic leadership should deploy targeted AI literacy and ethics training based on departmental needs 

 Conclusion:
AI is  a permanent part of student life. Students use AI tools for about 8 hours a week, especially in STEM and Business majors, and many even pay for premium versions out of their own pockets.
Because strict bans do not stop students from using AI, universities should stop trying to block these tools. Instead, schools need to teach students how to use AI responsibly, provide equal access so low-income students are not left behind, and check if heavy AI use is linked to student exam stress.

